---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import Receipt from '../components/Receipt.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';

// Every figure below is countable from the source repository. Nothing estimated.
const counts = [
  { n: '96', label: 'React components' },
  { n: '51', label: 'Storybook stories' },
  { n: '19', label: 'deployed applications' },
  { n: '6', label: 'rounds of review on running software' },
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="A design-to-code pipeline that returns working components"
  metaTitle="An AI-native design-to-code pipeline | Tyler Young"
  description="Replacing an agency handoff process with a Paper-to-Claude-Code pipeline producing production React, Tailwind and Storybook components."
  eyebrow="01 · AI-Native Practice"
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Director of Design · 90-day engagement · 2026'],
    ['Built', 'Design system v0 · Paper↔Claude Code pipeline'],
    ['Stack', 'React · Tailwind · Storybook · Vite · Fly.io'],
    ['Governed by', 'A 1,048-line operating spec'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      An agency sat between design intent and shipped UI, and every decision made a
      round trip through a handoff document. I replaced the document with a pipeline:
      Paper to Claude Code to React, Tailwind and Storybook, with the design system
      as the constraint on what could be generated. Stakeholder reviews moved onto
      running components.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <dl class="counts">
    {
      counts.map((c) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{c.n}</dt>
          <dd>{c.label}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>The design system</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The pipeline is a document. An operating spec that lets an autonomous agent
      build inside a real codebase without supervision it cannot safely be given —
      project boundaries, a command filter, secrets handling, an explicit list of
      what it may do alone. It decides what is allowed to exist, which is the part
      of a design system that actually holds.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Receipt
    label="workflow/CLAUDE.md — operating spec, 1,048 lines"
    leftLabel="On where my expertise ends"
    rightLabel="On what design judgment is worth automating"
    caption="Excerpts, verbatim. The full spec covers project boundaries, filesystem safety, secrets, deploy rules, and an explicit allowed / disallowed autonomy list."
  >
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><span class="dim">## Operating Context</span>

The user is a designer,
not a security engineer.

The user cannot reliably evaluate
terminal permissions or low-level
commands.

Therefore:

Claude must self-enforce safety rules
rather than relying on the user
to detect problems.

Claude should behave like:

<span class="cue">&gt;</span> a careful senior builder working
<span class="cue">&gt;</span> inside a small controlled workshop.</pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><span class="dim">## Autonomous QA Checklist — item 13</span>

Vertical lane alignment across
sections. Trace vertical lanes through
the full page. Do labels, values, and
indicators fall on shared column lines?

A structural mismatch is a layout bug
even if every individual property
within each section is correct.

<span class="cue">&gt;</span> Vertical alignment is foundational —
<span class="cue">&gt;</span> it is one of the dominant markers of
<span class="cue">&gt;</span> a well-designed page.</pre>
    </div>
  </Receipt>

  <h2>How it worked</h2>

  <h3>Paper, not Figma — because one returns code</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Paper's MCP returns actual JSX and Tailwind, not a description of a design to
      be translated. Figma stayed in the system as a reader for legacy files and
      nothing else. That single swap removes the interpretation step where most
      design-to-code fidelity is lost.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The rule that follows from it: every component begins as a literal copy of
      what the design tool returned. No reinterpreting absolute positioning as flex,
      no substituting spacing utilities for exact offsets. Abstraction is allowed
      only after the literal version passes review — because abstractions are
      opinions, and opinions have to be proven.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h3>The token system is the palette</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Every colour, weight, radius and spacing value has to trace to the project's
      token page. If the tokens are too narrow for what is being asked, the agent
      has to say so and wait rather than invent a value. Components are earned
      through design extraction — if a component has not appeared in a design, it
      does not belong in the library.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      That is what kept ninety-six components coherent without a second designer
      reviewing every commit.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h3>Options were built, not described</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Three directions for the intelligence layer were built and deployed to three
      separate URLs, so the choice was made against running software rather than in
      a review deck. The ops surface went through six iterations, each with an
      itemised feedback round against a live build.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Nineteen deployments across ninety days: six iterations of one surface, three
      parallel directions for another, the component library on its own URL, and the
      decision tools I used to run the engagement.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What the engagement produced</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A working pipeline and a v0 of the system it generates from — not a finished
      design system, and not the plan's targets restated as results. What can be
      checked is what is still running: the product, its component library, and
      the plan itself, all three at their own URLs and all three built inside the
      engagement.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Client screens are deliberately absent. The work contains a named end customer
      and their dealer-network data, and it has not been sanitised. Everything above
      is either my own writing or a count from the repository.
    </p>
  </div>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-built-culture-around-paradox',
        note: 'Ninety days buys a v0 and a proved process, not a finished system. Knowing which quality bars are load-bearing and which are vanity is the whole discipline of shipping inside a fixed window.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-translated-design-work',
        note: 'Replacing an agency is a budget argument before it is a design one. The pipeline had to be defensible in terms a stakeholder outside design could weigh — speed to production, and what stopped being paid for.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .counts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 22px 20px;
    margin: 0 0 40px;
    padding: 24px 0;
  }

  .counts div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .counts dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 40px);
    font-weight: 620;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .counts dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  @media (min-width: 810px) {
    .counts {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
